==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|londlord}}, from {{inh|en|ang|landhlāford}}, equivalent to {{af|en|land|lord}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|landlaird}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlænd.lɔːd/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlænd.lɔɹd/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-landlord.ogg|a=AU}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A person that leases [[real property]]; a [[lessor]].
#: {{syn|en|lessor|lease provider|q3=informal|leaser}}
#: {{ant|en|tenant|lessee}}
#: {{hypo|en|sublessor|underlessor|sublandlord|underlandlord|subletter|underletter|q7=informal|subleaser|underleaser}}
#* {{RQ:Jack Straw|I|18|Brethren, brethren, it were better to haue this communitie,<br>Then to haue this difference in degrees:<br>The '''landlord''' his rent, the lawyer his fees.<br>So quickly the poore mans ſubſtance is ſpent {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|British}} The [[owner]] or [[manager]] of a [[public house]].
# {{lb|en|surfing|slang|with "the"}} A [[shark]], imagined as the owner of the surf to be avoided.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2004|author=Drew Kampion; publisher's blurb for|title=Stories from the Surf &ndash; The Lost Coast|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061025052557/http://secure.realsurf.com/oscommerce/catalog/product_info.php?products_id=146
|passage=''the lurking presence of “The '''Landlord'''”''}}

